 * Build system
   * MinGW32 GCC > 4.1.2 will crash on loading linux.sys, because object divdi3
     contains debug symbols (.debug_line, .debug_ranges) in relocation tables.

 * Kernel:
   * Merge coLinux kernel patch with official kernels paravirt_ops.
     "lguest" in kernel code is a good start point.
   * SSE/MMX support is disabled by bits X86_FEATURE_XMM/MMX in cpu caps.
     Problem is, that inside usage of sse/mmx registers we can not allow
     an OS switch, example for page allocations. This ends with an endless
     FPU fault, see bug#2524658.

 * COFB:
   * Merge with covideo.
   * Implement mouse handling X module, ref. vbmouse in vbox.
   * Make screen scroll working.
   * Test linux host cofb.
   * Copy from X window then paste to host.
   * Seamless window ref. vbox addon

 * Consoles:
   * Focus change handling.
     If a key is pressed and only released after changing window focus, it
     will stay pressed forever until it is pressed/released again.
     This can be solved by handling FOCUS_EVENT in the input loop (just
     release all pressed keys, like in the FLTK code).
   * Some more accurate console shortcuts handling.
     I'm not exactly sure of what needs to be done here, but I think we can
     solve it by using the modifier flags in the key input record.
   * Either support multiple consoles or stop the second from attaching
     (probably the latter first). Any console can attach now, broking the
     first one (and all others, off course).
   * Make the console resizable.
   * Implement backlog in NT consoles (shift+PgUp). I think it can be easy to
     do in the NT console, as the OS already gives us a screen buffer.
   * Implement setfont in the FLTK console (use the kernel fonts). Needs
     refactoring and maybe some hard work.

 * Installer:
   * Instruct the user to install Python for Windows, wxPython, and Python 
     Win32 extensions (or use py2exe).
   * The installer should add have an option to launch the configurator 
     when the installation finishes. 

 * Configurator:
   * Finish implementation of editing configuration files.
   * On the place block devices are configured, add the ability to 
     download the images from sourceforge.
   * On the place where network devices are configured, the configurator
     will enumerate either currently installed network adapter and TAP
     devices. More user friendliness could be added there to support the
     installation of one or more TAP devices.
   * Add nice wizards for the newbies.

 * Operating system ports:
   * Create build environment, driver and daemons for 64 bit Windows.
   * Upgrade Linux as host for more recent kernels, and for 64 bit.
   * Continue OSX port.

 * Miscellaneous enhancements and fixes:
   * Lack of entropy - empty /dev/random
   * Make coLinux work on Windows NT 4 SP*.
   * Address all reported SMP issues.
   * The first search path for files specified in the XML, such as in 
     <image path="vmlinux" /> or block devices, should be in the directory 
     that contains the XML file.
   * Revise the argument parsing of the executables other than daemon, i.e,
     use the framework that the daemon uses.
   * Support to shutdown/suspend/resume (just!) coLinux so it becomes
     transportable between hosts.
   * Import features from UML

 * conet: 
   * To fix: If the host connection is lost and returned, colinux-daemon will
     not automatically return conet.
   * Possibly WinPCAP related, but a fix for support of wireless networks
     would be great.
   * Possibly WinPCAP related, compatibility with IPv6: IPv6 can be
     obtained but setting the IPv6 host address based on routeradvertisements
     is not working fine (I can see IPv6 traffic though in coLinux).

 * Root file system images: 
   * Post more images on SourceForge. Send me URLs of ready images. I will
     not accept images that are tied to specific coLinux releases.

 * Odd things to investigate:
   * Some iptables functionality not working? (see ML)
   * Spurious 'Floating Point Exception' with an athlon optimised vmlinux.

 * Guest kernel config:
   * CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK is on as of 2026-08-02 and pacman no longer needs
     --disable-sandbox. It cost two symbols -- SECURITY_LANDLOCK and the
     SECURITY_PATH it depends on -- and CONFIG_LSM already listed landlock
     first, so nothing else had to change. The guest says "landlock: Up and
     running." at boot and `pacman -Sy` returns 0 with the alpm sandbox active.
     Note that /sys/kernel/security does not exist in this guest, so the usual
     way of checking (cat /sys/kernel/security/lsm) reports nothing and looks
     like failure; securityfs is simply not mounted. The dmesg line is the
     check.
   * The guest .config is not in the tree and nothing reproduces it. Every
     option this port depends on -- KASAN off because the host's allocation
     lands in Linux's shadow region, RANDOMIZE_MEMORY off because the KASLR
     span encloses our window, DEBUG_ENTRY off because it ud2's on the real IF
     this design keeps set, and now SECURITY_LANDLOCK on -- is a decision made
     once and recorded nowhere a rebuild would find it.
   * "x86/mm: Checked W+X mappings: FAILED, 125045 W+X pages found" on every
     boot, from the kernel's own page-table auditor. The host builds the direct
     map with _PAGE_RW and no NX, so every page of it is writable and
     executable. It is harmless for a guest that runs at ring 0 anyway -- it has
     no privilege boundary to protect -- but nothing in the tree says so, and it
     reads like a security finding to whoever sees it next. Either mark the map
     NX where the guest never executes, or write down why it is not worth doing.

 * The Arch root filesystem is not reproducible:
   * tools/mkrootfs.sh builds the busybox root (root.img) and nothing builds
     root-arch.img. That image is a bootstrap tarball plus a pile of changes
     made by hand through the terminal: the pacman keyring initialised and
     populated (pacman-key --init && --populate archlinux, without which every
     package fails signature verification as TRUST_UNDEFINED even though the
     signature is good), a Server line in /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist, the systemd
     OSC-context symlink removed and its tmpfiles snippet masked,
     /etc/systemd/network/10-eth0.network, and systemd-networkd enabled. None of
     that survives losing the image.
   * So: a mkarchrootfs.sh that goes from download/archlinux-bootstrap to a
     bootable image with all of the above applied. Note while writing it that
     the image cannot be pushed to the box afterwards -- 1.2 GB through the
     transfer agent OOMs a 4 GB machine, because the agent buffers and deploy.sh
     then reads it back to verify. Build it there or accept a reboot.
   * And a rule that cost an evening: changes made inside a running guest are
     only on disk after a clean poweroff. stop.bat and the run deadline do not
     unmount, so a file written seconds earlier comes back as a journaled inode
     with no data -- zero bytes, which looks like the write never happened.

 * The box-side scripts are not in the tree:
   * deploy.sh, run-arch.bat, run-linux.bat, console.bat, net.bat, stop.bat,
     reboot.bat, dbg-start.bat, verifier-on/off.bat and getdumps.bat all live in
     dist-x64/, which is outside the repository. Every one of them encodes
     something learned the hard way -- why the debug stream must be launched
     detached, why shutdown.exe has to be called by full path, why a deploy
     verifies by reading files back -- and none of it is version controlled.
     tools/harden-m92p.bat is in the tree; the rest should be too.

 * KREAD has no lock against teardown:
   * CO_MANAGER_IOCTL_KREAD walks the guest's page tables with no protection
     against co_kload_free destroying the address space underneath it. It is
     safe today only because its one caller is the boot daemon itself,
     sequenced before its own KLOAD_END. Any second process using it would be
     the use-after-free that the CONET path needed net_lock to avoid. Either
     give it the same retire-under-lock treatment or document that it is
     single-caller by construction.

 * i386 does not build:
   * src/colinux/kernel/kload.c uses CO_ARCH_PGD_INDEX and _PAGE_GLOBAL, which
     exist only in arch/x86_64/mmu.h, so the i386 target has not compiled since
     f2ed1128. Nothing in this port needs i386, but "the 32-bit build still
     works" was a stated property of the early milestones and it quietly stopped
     being true. Either exclude the x86-64-only kernel loader from that build or
     drop the claim.

 * Consoles (this port's, not the 2004 ones):
   * colinux-daemon --console serves one client at a time: listen(backlog 1) and
     a single accept loop, so a second connection is reset rather than refused
     with a reason, and there is no way to look at a guest somebody else is
     already attached to.
   * colinux-console-nt cannot attach to an x86-64 guest at all. It opens a
     monitor instance (co_user_monitor_open with CO_MODULE_CONSOLE) and expects
     screen-buffer updates from the guest's coconsole driver; this port creates
     no monitor instance and the guest uses hvc, which is a byte stream. Making
     it work means either porting the monitor path and coconsole, or teaching it
     to read the CONSOLE ioctl and parse VT sequences itself.

 * Guest RAM is freed and re-allocated every run, and that fragments the host:
   * The symptom, which is worth stating first because it looks like a driver
     bug and is not: the first boot after `sc start` is reliable, and a second
     boot on the same loaded driver makes the whole box sluggish or wedges it
     outright, from the instant the daemon starts and before the guest executes
     an instruction. A reboot cures it every time and nothing else does.
   * The cause is that co_kload_begin opens with co_kload_free, so every run
     hands its guest RAM back to Windows and the next one asks for all of it
     again. At 2 GB in 32 MB pieces that is around sixty-four
     MmAllocateContiguousMemory calls per boot, each a search of the PFN
     database for an unbroken physical run, with the memory manager's locks held
     and working-set trimming when it cannot find one.
     On a freshly booted host physical memory is one long stretch and those
     searches are cheap. After a run has cycled a gigabyte or two through the
     contiguous allocator the address space is holed, and the same sixty-four
     searches have to hunt for runs that no longer exist in one piece. So the
     state that is not reset between runs is not ours -- it is Windows'
     physical memory, and we are the ones fragmenting it.
   * The targeted fix is to stop giving it back. Allocate the guest's RAM once
     per driver load and reuse it across runs: nothing else can use those blocks
     while the driver is loaded, one guest runs at a time by construction, and
     the second and tenth boots then cost zero contiguous searches. co_kload_free
     keeps the blocks and destroys only the address space; a new
     co_kload_release_memory drops them from the manager's unload path, where
     co_kload_free is already called.
   * Three things that have to be got right, and the reason this was not done
     in passing. Block 0 holds the image and the page tables, so a reused block
     needs kload_table_top reset and a freshly built space rather than the old
     one. The previous run's RAM contents survive into the next boot, which is
     harmless only because .bss arrives as explicit zeroing chunks from the
     loader -- if that ever stops being true, a guest inherits the last one's
     memory. And this is the teardown path, which has produced a bugcheck 0xC2
     (double free, fixed with kload_lock) and a host freeze (per-entry
     MmUnmapIoSpace in co_space_free_table) in a single evening; it deserves its
     own change and its own five-point audit rather than a rider on something
     else.
   * The real fix underneath is pseudo-physical memory. The contiguity
     requirement exists only because guest physical must equal host physical,
     which is forced by Linux calling __va() on entries it reads out of its own
     page tables. A p2m/machine-frame layer -- which coLinux-i386 and Xen both
     have -- removes it entirely, and with it the whole class of problem: RAM
     becomes ordinary scattered pages, there is no e820 entry limit to work
     around, and CO_KLOAD_MAX_BLOCKS stops existing. That is a milestone, not a
     fix.

 * One-click bring-up:
   * Starting a networked guest currently takes three windows on the box and
     four commands inside it: run-arch.bat, console.bat, net.bat, and then
     ip link / ip addr / ip route / resolv.conf by hand. Every one of those is
     a step that can be forgotten or done in the wrong order, and the failure
     when the guest has no address is "cannot resolve host", which reads as a
     DNS or a slirp problem rather than a missing step.
   * The guest half is done: /etc/systemd/network/10-eth0.network plus
     systemd-networkd enabled, so a booted guest addresses itself. That lives
     in root-arch.img, which means it is not reproducible from the tree --
     tools/mkrootfs.sh should write it when it builds an image, or there should
     be a documented way to reapply it.
   * The host half wants one script that installs the driver if it is not
     installed, refuses if a guest is already running (run-boot.sh has that
     guard; run-arch.bat does not, and going around it cost a box), starts the
     debug stream, then launches the boot, the console and the net bridge in
     their own windows. `start /min` per daemon, in the order that does not
     wedge the transfer agent -- see dbg-start.bat's notes on why the stream
     must be launched detached and never waited on.
   * And the two things that still need a flag or an edit before pacman works:
     CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK in the guest kernel (pacman 7 sandboxes downloads
     and refuses without it), and a Server line in
     /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist, which the Arch bootstrap ships entirely
     commented out.

 * An unexplained #GP in trap_init, seen once:
   * A Manjaro boot stopped with vector 13 at co_colinux_adopt_traps+0x150,
     error code 0x40. The error code is a selector, not an address: index 8 in
     the GDT, which on x86-64 is GDT_ENTRY_TSS, so the processor refused the ltr
     that loads the guest's task register. The kernel log ends at the
     inode-cache line, which is vfs_caches_init_early() -- the call immediately
     before trap_init() -- so the stopping point and the faulting address agree.
   * It has not recurred, on the same vmlinux, across many boots since. That is
     not the same as fixed, and it is recorded here rather than forgotten
     because an intermittent fault in the one function that installs the guest's
     descriptor tables is the kind of thing that comes back during a
     demonstration. Candidates worth reading when it does: whether the TSS
     descriptor's busy bit can be set on entry (the switch clears it before
     every ltr in both directions, but adopt_traps does its own lgdt/ltr/lidt
     outside that), and whether the descriptor's base can be built from a
     per-CPU TSS address before the guest has settled which CPU it is on.

 * The release artifacts are not built by anything:
   * The user-facing deliverable is meant to be a download and one executable --
     no cross toolchain, no pacman, no Linux machine. That means the driver, the
     daemons, vmlinux and root-manjaro.img all have to be produced by a build
     step somebody can run, and today only the first three are, by hand.
   * tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh builds the image and runs anywhere with pacman and
     e2fsprogs -- including inside the guest, which is how the current one was
     made, because a 6 GB image cannot be pushed to the test box. It has never
     been run on a build host, which is the case that matters for shipping, and
     that is the one thing standing between "reproducible" and "reproducible by
     someone else".
   * The image also has to get smaller before it can ship. 6 GB with about a
     gigabyte used is mostly zeroes, so zstd takes it to a few hundred
     megabytes; the launcher then has to expand it on first run, which is
     another thing the single exe should own.

 * The guest kernel configuration is not in the tree:
   * Nothing records which options this port needs, so a vmlinux built from
     patch/7.1.5/current-tree-snapshot.diff and x86_64_defconfig is missing
     things whose absence is invisible until userspace fails oddly:
     CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK (pacman 7 sandboxes its downloads and refuses to
     fetch anything without it), CONFIG_USER_NS (Steam, bwrap, flatpak and
     Chromium all want unprivileged user namespaces), and the negatives that are
     load-bearing -- KASAN off because the host's allocation lands in PML4 slot
     501 inside Linux's KASAN shadow, RANDOMIZE_MEMORY off because the KASLR
     span encloses our window, DEBUG_ENTRY off because it ud2's on the real IF
     at the exception return paths, which is the bit this design keeps set.
   * A config fragment plus the scripts/config invocations that apply it, so the
     kernel is reproducible from the tree rather than from whatever is in
     build/linux-7.1.5-coop/.config on one machine.

 * Debug daemon, after networking and pacman -S work:
   * It needs a clean shutdown. `colinux-debug-daemon -d` holds a handle on
     \\.\CoLinuxDriver forever and blocks inside a co_debug_read ioctl waiting
     for records, so there is no way to ask it to stop. `sc stop
     CoLinuxDriver` then reports success and parks the service in
     STOP_PENDING: the I/O manager will not call DriverUnload while a handle
     is open, and the driver stays resident and half-stopped.

     That leaves only two ways to swap a driver, and both are worse than the
     problem. taskkill ends the process but cannot interrupt its thread while
     that thread is inside the driver, so the handles close afterwards and the
     driver is released underneath kernel-mode code -- which is precisely the
     bugcheck 0x50 in the handle-close path that took this box down four times
     in one evening. Or reboot, which is what we do, at a couple of minutes
     each and several times a session.

     What it wants is a way to be asked: an event the daemon waits on
     alongside the ring, or a second ioctl that makes the blocking read
     return, so it can close its handle and exit on request. Then
     reload-driver.sh stops needing to kill anything, and `sc stop` means what
     it says.

 * A 0x50 in ExFreePool at end-of-run, not root-caused:
   * On 2026-08-02 a teardown bugchecked 0x50 with the faulting instruction at
     ntoskrnl+0xaf8a3, reached through dispatch_wrapper -> co_manager_ioctl ->
     co_arch_boot_loaded -> co_arch_extable_free. That is ExFreePool reading
     pool metadata for the 18 KB exception table, so the block's header was
     already corrupt when it was freed -- something wrote into pool near it.
     co_arch_extable_free nulls after freeing, so a double free of that pointer
     is not the mechanism, and the table itself is unchanged at 1516 entries in
     every run before and after.
   * It has not reproduced since, and the previous 0x50 series in this port was
     explicitly intermittent. Special pool does not cover it: Driver Verifier
     only special-pools allocations of a page or less, and this is 18 KB, so an
     overrun into it is invisible until the header is read at free time. Some
     way of catching the write rather than the free is what this needs.
   * logs/repro.sh drives boot / pacman -Sy / poweroff cycles looking for it. It
     must never kill a process to clean up between cycles -- see the note at the
     top of it, which cost a box.

 * co_arch_guest_space_destroy walks tables it does not own:
   * It reports "guest space: freed N tables, owned M" whenever the two
     disagree, and they have disagreed on every teardown in the history of this
     port: 33/32, 35/32, 42/32, 46/34, and 62/64 and 63/65 where it freed fewer
     than it owns. Nobody read the line.
   * freed > owned because the walk descends into subtrees the space did not
     create -- the kernel's own level3_kernel_pgt, and the three host grafts at
     PML4 slots 273, 289 and 501. freed < owned because tables the space did
     create are no longer reachable from the top by the time it walks, the
     guest having replaced entries with its own. Neither is fatal today: with a
     frame source set nothing is actually put back, and the pages all live
     inside the blocks that kload_release_pages frees wholesale.
   * The fix is to record which top-level entries were grafted and not descend
     into them, which also stops the walk touching host page tables with
     MmMapIoSpace at all.

 * Nothing can ask the console or the network bridge to stop:
   * Same shape as the debug daemon entry above and worth solving together. The
     bridge is documented to keep waiting when there is no guest, and the
     console is another colinux-daemon.exe, so after a guest powers off both
     are still there and "is a colinux-daemon running" cannot tell them from a
     boot. That ambiguity is what made an automated repro loop reach for
     taskkill, and taskkill is what OOMed the box.
   * colinux-daemon.exe --install-driver reports "driver already installed /
     daemon: driver installed" and exits 0 when the service exists but is
     STOPPED, which is its state after every reboot. It does not start it.
     Anything that treats that success as "the driver is loaded" then fails
     with "manager open: The system cannot find the file specified" on every
     subsequent run. It should start the service, or say that it did not.

 * Cleanups:
   * Merge console into console-base.
   * Clear out some function naming brain damage and make more use
     of the 'static' keyword :).
   * The usual tidbits: comments, refactoring, etc.
